1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,321
Last lecture's loop had one box doing all the interesting work: act, call a tool.

2
00:00:05,321 --> 00:00:10,475
Here's exactly what that box contains, because "the AI checked my calendar" is doing

3
00:00:10,475 --> 00:00:14,096
a lot of hidden mechanical work you should be able to name.

4
00:00:14,096 --> 00:00:15,937
Two things make this possible.

5
00:00:15,937 --> 00:00:21,091
Function calling is how a model asks for something specific instead of just talking.

6
00:00:21,091 --> 00:00:26,246
MCP is how it reaches your actual tools, Workspace, Notion, Lark, whatever your team

7
00:00:26,246 --> 00:00:29,989
runs on, without someone hand-wiring every single connection.

8
00:00:29,989 --> 00:00:34,960
Neither one is exotic once you see it plainly, and that's the actual goal of this

9
00:00:34,960 --> 00:00:35,451
lecture.

10
00:00:35,451 --> 00:00:40,114
Not to make you able to build either, but to make sure nobody can wave "it's

11
00:00:40,114 --> 00:00:44,901
connected via MCP" at you as though that settles a question it hasn't actually

12
00:00:44,901 --> 00:00:45,699
answered yet.

13
00:00:46,249 --> 00:00:50,890
That's the whole mechanism, and the detail worth sitting with is the middle box.

14
00:00:50,890 --> 00:00:53,269
The model doesn't write and execute code.

15
00:00:53,269 --> 00:00:57,852
It emits a structured request, a function name and some arguments, formatted so

16
00:00:57,852 --> 00:00:59,070
software can read it.

17
00:00:59,070 --> 00:01:03,711
Your system is the one that actually calls the calendar, checks the result makes

18
00:01:03,711 --> 00:01:08,468
sense, and hands the answer back as text the model reads like anything else in its

19
00:01:08,468 --> 00:01:08,933
context.

20
00:01:08,933 --> 00:01:13,806
So where do you actually get to enforce the rules that matter: which functions exist

21
00:01:13,806 --> 00:01:18,621
at all, what arguments are allowed, whether this particular action needs a human to

22
00:01:18,621 --> 00:01:19,607
approve it first?

23
00:01:19,607 --> 00:01:21,522
Right there, in that middle step.

24
00:01:21,522 --> 00:01:22,624
The model requests.

25
00:01:22,624 --> 00:01:24,829
Your system decides whether to comply.

26
00:01:24,829 --> 00:01:29,528
Losing sight of that boundary is how a demo video convinces a room that the model

27
00:01:29,528 --> 00:01:33,067
has more direct access to your systems than it actually does.

28
00:01:33,067 --> 00:01:37,012
Extend the loop one step further and the picture gets more concrete.

29
00:01:37,012 --> 00:01:41,769
Say the model checked the calendar, found a free slot, and now wants to schedule a

30
00:01:41,769 --> 00:01:42,233
meeting.

31
00:01:42,233 --> 00:01:46,643
That's a second function call, "create_event", with its own arguments: time,

32
00:01:46,643 --> 00:01:48,673
attendees, a title it wrote itself.

33
00:01:48,673 --> 00:01:53,140
Reading a calendar and creating an event look identical from the chat window.

34
00:01:53,140 --> 00:01:57,839
They are not identical requests, and treating them as though they were is exactly

35
00:01:57,839 --> 00:01:59,986
the gap this lecture exists to close.

36
00:02:00,536 --> 00:02:05,361
Before this protocol existed, every one of those rows meant an engineer writing

37
00:02:05,361 --> 00:02:10,370
bespoke code to talk to that specific product's API, then maintaining it when that

38
00:02:10,370 --> 00:02:12,080
API changed underneath them.

39
00:02:12,080 --> 00:02:16,723
MCP standardises the middle of that picture: one protocol an agent speaks to

40
00:02:16,723 --> 00:02:21,853
discover what a tool offers and call it, the same way regardless of whether the tool

41
00:02:21,853 --> 00:02:23,686
is Workspace, Notion, or Lark.

42
00:02:23,686 --> 00:02:27,229
It doesn't remove the work of connecting to a real system.

43
00:02:27,229 --> 00:02:32,237
It means that work gets done once, by whoever maintains that connector, instead of

44
00:02:32,237 --> 00:02:34,742
once per project that happens to need it.

45
00:02:34,742 --> 00:02:39,323
That's the actual shift worth understanding, not the name of any particular

46
00:02:39,323 --> 00:02:41,522
connector, which will keep changing.

47
00:02:41,522 --> 00:02:44,454
What it doesn't standardise is worth naming too.

48
00:02:44,454 --> 00:02:49,585
A protocol says nothing about whether a specific connector was built carefully, what

49
00:02:49,585 --> 00:02:54,532
it logs, or whether the person who installed it read the permission scopes before

50
00:02:54,532 --> 00:02:55,448
approving them.

51
00:02:55,448 --> 00:03:00,213
Two calendar connectors can speak identical MCP and still differ completely on

52
00:03:00,213 --> 00:03:02,961
whether a mistaken write is even recoverable.

53
00:03:02,961 --> 00:03:06,871
Standard plumbing is not the same claim as trustworthy plumbing.

54
00:03:07,421 --> 00:03:12,283
Say the honest caveat, because "connect it to Workspace" sounds like one decision

55
00:03:12,283 --> 00:03:13,724
and is actually several.

56
00:03:13,724 --> 00:03:16,125
Read access to a calendar is low stakes.

57
00:03:16,125 --> 00:03:21,048
Write access, the ability to create events, send email, or edit a live document on

58
00:03:21,048 --> 00:03:26,031
its own, is a completely different risk, and it's easy to grant more than you meant

59
00:03:26,031 --> 00:03:29,873
to because the connector ships with both switched on by default.

60
00:03:29,873 --> 00:03:34,795
Before any tool connection goes live, ask specifically what it can write, not just

61
00:03:34,795 --> 00:03:39,358
what it can read, and whether every write needs a human to approve it first.

62
00:03:39,358 --> 00:03:44,160
That question is this section's guardrails lecture arriving four lectures early,

63
00:03:44,160 --> 00:03:46,802
because it genuinely starts here, not there.

64
00:03:46,802 --> 00:03:49,803
Picture the ordinary way this actually goes wrong.

65
00:03:49,803 --> 00:03:54,786
Someone approves Workspace access for an agent so it can "check a few calendars," a

66
00:03:54,786 --> 00:03:57,067
perfectly reasonable-sounding request.

67
00:03:57,067 --> 00:04:01,870
The connector they installed defaults to read and write on every calendar it can

68
00:04:01,870 --> 00:04:04,752
see, because that's the simpler product to ship.

69
00:04:04,752 --> 00:04:08,173
Nobody meant to grant write access to the CEO's calendar.

70
00:04:08,173 --> 00:04:11,835
Nobody looked closely enough at the scope to notice they had.

71
00:04:12,385 --> 00:04:17,096
That's the definition worth keeping, and notice what it doesn't promise: nothing

72
00:04:17,096 --> 00:04:21,277
about which specific tools exist, because that list changes constantly.

73
00:04:21,277 --> 00:04:25,693
What doesn't change is the shape of the question you ask about any of them.

74
00:04:25,693 --> 00:04:29,226
Try the exercise on a real proposal, not a hypothetical one.

75
00:04:29,226 --> 00:04:34,172
A vague answer to "which functions can it call" is the same gap as a vague answer to

76
00:04:34,172 --> 00:04:36,646
acceptance criteria back in Section three.

77
00:04:36,646 --> 00:04:41,062
Someone hasn't actually decided yet, and shipping before they do is how the

78
00:04:41,062 --> 00:04:42,769
write-access mistake happens.

79
00:04:42,769 --> 00:04:46,538
Notice this exercise doesn't require you to read a line of code.

80
00:04:46,538 --> 00:04:51,190
The function list is a product decision before it's an engineering one: someone

81
00:04:51,190 --> 00:04:55,901
chose to expose "create_event" instead of just "check_calendar", and that choice

82
00:04:55,901 --> 00:05:00,847
deserves the same scrutiny you'd give any other feature decision, not a pass because

83
00:05:00,847 --> 00:05:02,790
the word agent is attached to it.

84
00:05:03,340 --> 00:05:05,107
One thing before you move on.

85
00:05:05,107 --> 00:05:08,520
Make the list itself, split cleanly into read and write.

86
00:05:08,520 --> 00:05:13,639
Most people asked this question for the first time find the write column longer than

87
00:05:13,639 --> 00:05:14,492
they expected.

88
00:05:14,492 --> 00:05:19,611
Then ask the harder one: who actually approved that access, and what the system does

89
00:05:19,611 --> 00:05:23,816
the moment a call goes wrong, wrong data, wrong recipient, wrong day.

90
00:05:23,816 --> 00:05:28,691
Next lecture gives you the maths for how often "wrong" shows up once an agent is

91
00:05:28,691 --> 00:05:33,445
chaining several of these calls together in a row, and it's a less comfortable

92
00:05:33,445 --> 00:05:35,334
number than most demos suggest.
